Show the windows that come after the first: a question an application asks, a file chooser, a
second window. Before this, a window that drew its picture once and then waited was never in
the frame and could not be reached by the pointer, although it had the keyboard.

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of gst-wayland-display-render-on-commit.patch,
gst-wayland-display-cursor-messages.patch and gst-wayland-display-windows.patch in this
directory, in that order. The lines of context are from that project and from those patches.

It is kept small and in a shape that can be proposed to that project
(docs/adr/0009-host-compositor.md). The rule for where a window goes is in
wayland-display-core/src/comp/windows.rs with its tests. There is no property: all of it is
always on, because what it mends is a fault, and nothing changes for an application that has
one window.

The fault: a new toplevel is put into the picture in the handler of the commit that brings
its first buffer, but a window was asked for its size (`Window::on_commit`) only if it was in
the picture already when that handler began. So a window was shown with a size of 0 by 0 and
learned its size at its NEXT commit. A window that keeps drawing commits again at once; one
that draws once and waits for its user never does.

What it changes:

- The size is taken at the commit that brings the first buffer.
- A toplevel with a parent (`xdg_toplevel.set_parent`: a dialog) is not told to fill the
  output. It is told how much room there is (the configure's bounds), keeps the size it
  wants, and is put over the middle of its parent, moved as far as it takes to lie inside the
  output. One that is larger than the output begins at the output's corner, and what does not
  fit is not seen. A toplevel without a parent is treated as before: told to fill the output
  (unless it has a largest size) and put at the corner, above what is there.
- A window that comes has the keyboard, as before. When a toplevel goes, it is taken out of
  the picture at once, the keyboard goes to the window that is on top then, and a frame is
  made in the "render on commit" mode.
- Pointer events go to the window under the pointer where it IS. Before, a motion was sent to
  the window under the place the pointer had left, so that a click after one jump went to the
  wrong window; with one window that fills the output the two are the same. When a window
  comes up over another or goes, the pointer is told again where it is without having moved.
- A click on a window puts the windows that have it as their parent above it again.

Tests (`cargo test -p wayland-display-core`): two of the rule for the place, and six with a
real Wayland client that opens a second toplevel which commits exactly once: without a parent
it is in the frame and takes a click; with a parent it keeps its size, lies over the middle,
and has the pointer that stood there; it has the keyboard and hands it, and the pointer, back
when it goes; a click on the parent leaves it in front; one larger than the output begins at
the corner and a frame is still made; two ordinary windows both fill the output with the
later in front.

Known limits:

- All windows are in one picture. Two ordinary windows of one application lie on top of each
  other, each as large as the output; the earlier one is reached again when the later goes.
  There is no way to switch between them from outside yet, and no message says where each
  window lies (the `wayland.windows` message of the third patch could carry that).
- A dialog is not kept from losing the keyboard to its parent when the parent is clicked
  beside it; it stays in front.
- The parent is looked at when the window gets its first buffer. A parent named later does
  not move the window.
- A window is placed once. It is not placed again when it changes its size or when the
  output does.

